## 1. Recomputed call-site set

Method: whole-file text search (`grep -n lowerArrayBoundary`), not boundary guessing.

```
746:  boundaryResult = lowerArrayBoundary(init, v.t);
2567: return lowerArrayBoundary(inner, target, actualOptional, destinationOptionalOverride).text;
2578: function lowerArrayBoundary(...)              <-- definition, not a call
2627: final targetValue = lowerArrayBoundary(nilMerge.target, target, null, destinationOptionalOverride);
2628: final fallbackValue = lowerArrayBoundary(nilMerge.fallback, target, null, destinationOptionalOverride);
2642: final trueValue = lowerArrayBoundary(whenTrue, target, null, destinationOptionalOverride);
2643: final falseValue = lowerArrayBoundary(whenFalse, target, null, destinationOptionalOverride);
4760: final prepared = lowerArrayBoundary(args[i], p, null, true);
4863: final prepared = lowerArrayBoundary(args[i], p, null, true);
```

Count: **9 occurrences = 8 call sites (746, 2567, 2627, 2628, 2642, 2643, 4760, 4863) + 1 definition (2578).**
This is an exact match with the claim. No 9th call site, no 10th. Repo-wide search over
`packages tests samples examples tools reference` returns exactly one file: `SwiftExpr.hx`.
Raw: `evidence/caller-set.txt`.

A note the claim does not make: three of the eight (746, 4760, 4863) are *consumer* sites that
decide for a destination, while five (2567, 2627, 2628, 2642, 2643) are internal recursion or the
`arrayBoundaryText` wrapper. Site 2567 is the only entry point reachable from `armLines`.

